\subsection{From analysis to a candidate sea-state schedule}
\label{sec:riccati-similarity-design-guidance}

The pure regularization analysis of Sec.~\ref{sec:integral-regularization-theory}
and the sea-state similarity arguments above constrain a sensible scheduler
without uniquely determining it.  Three ideas survive the model reductions.
First, $\sigma_{aw}\tau^3$ is the natural scale of the third-integral state.
Second, scaling the pseudo-update period with $\tau$ keeps the number of
regularization updates per OU correlation time approximately fixed.  Third,
regularization is a drift--distortion compromise rather than covariance matching
to a true sensor.  These principles motivated the implementation family that
was then calibrated and tested with the complete MEKF.

\subsubsection{Candidate wrapper law}
\label{sec:riccati-deployed-cadence}

The deployed wrapper forms the cubic base target
\begin{equation}
r_S^{\mathrm{base}}=c_R\sigma_{aw}\tau^3,
\end{equation}
and scales the pseudo-update cadence with the applied OU time scale,
\begin{equation}
T_S=\clip(c_T\tau_{\mathrm{appl}},T_{S,\min},T_{S,\max}).
\end{equation}
To preserve the historical pseudo-measurement information rate when that cadence
moves, the standard deviation passed to the filter is
\begin{equation}
r_{S,\mathrm{filter}}
=r_S^{\mathrm{base}}\sqrt{\frac{T_{S,0}}{T_S}}.
\label{eq:deployed-rs-summary}
\end{equation}
Away from clamps, this produces the implemented filter-input dependence
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
r_{S,\mathrm{filter}}\propto\sigma_{aw}\tau^{5/2}.}
\label{eq:deployed-effective-law}
\end{equation}
Equation~\eqref{eq:deployed-effective-law} is an engineering schedule, not the
closed-form optimum of Eq.~\eqref{eq:reduced-optimal-rs}.  In particular, the
reduced MSE theorem contains the true-motion moment $m_{-4}$ and a coupled
$q_{\mathrm{eff}}$ that are not available as independent online quantities in
the deployed MEKF.  The scheduler therefore uses the directly estimated wave
time scale and acceleration level, applies bounded smoothing, and calibrates its
coefficient from full-filter experiments.

\subsubsection{Empirical amplitude choice}
\label{sec:riccati-law-ablation}

The implementation exposes the amplitude dependence independently of the common
time-scale factor.  The paired exponent ablation reported with the validation
study finds that retaining a linear dependence on $\sigma_{aw}$ performs best
among the tested amplitude exponents.  This is evidence for the engineering
schedule over the evaluated sea family; it does not identify a unique physical
decomposition of the full estimator's low-frequency acceleration error into
sensor noise, attitude leakage, residual bias, and model mismatch.

The coefficients, bounds, averaging horizons, and one-sample staging used by
the actual scheduler are specified in Sec.~\ref{sec:adaptation}.  Keeping that
implementation description separate from the reduced analysis makes the claim
hierarchy explicit: theory identifies the mechanism and useful scaling
variables; complete-estimator experiments select and validate the deployed
schedule.
